@module flutter-m02 | Flutter Fundamentals
@summary Widgets, stateless and stateful widgets, the widget tree, BuildContext, lifecycle, hot reload, MaterialApp and Scaffold.

@lesson flutter-m02-l01 | Widgets | 12
## Learning objectives
- Explain what a widget is and why widgets are immutable
- Compose small widgets into larger ones
- Recognise layout, visual and interactive widgets

## Widgets are descriptions
A **widget** is an immutable description of part of the user interface. It doesn't *draw* anything itself; it's a lightweight configuration object that Flutter uses to create and update elements and render objects (Module 1).

```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

class PriceTag extends StatelessWidget {
  const PriceTag({super.key, required this.amount});
  final double amount;

  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    return Container(
      padding: const EdgeInsets.symmetric(horizontal: 12, vertical: 6),
      decoration: BoxDecoration(
        color: Colors.green.shade100,
        borderRadius: BorderRadius.circular(20),
      ),
      child: Text(
        '\$${amount.toStringAsFixed(2)}',
        style: const TextStyle(fontWeight: FontWeight.bold),
      ),
    );
  }
}
```

`PriceTag` is a custom widget made by **composing** `Container`, `BoxDecoration` (a description, not a widget) and `Text`.

## Immutability
All widget fields are `final`. To show something different, Flutter builds a **new** widget with new values. Because widgets are cheap, this is fast, and immutability makes UI predictable: a widget always looks the same for the same configuration.

## Kinds of widgets
- **Layout**: `Row`, `Column`, `Stack`, `Padding`, `Center`, `Expanded`, `ListView`
- **Visual**: `Text`, `Image`, `Icon`, `Container`, `Card`, `DecoratedBox`
- **Interactive**: `ElevatedButton`, `TextField`, `Checkbox`, `GestureDetector`, `InkWell`
- **Structural**: `MaterialApp`, `Scaffold`, `AppBar`, `Navigator`
- **Inherited/state**: `Theme`, `MediaQuery`, and widgets that provide data to descendants

## Composition over configuration
Flutter widgets do one thing each. Instead of a `Text` with a `padding` property, you wrap the `Text` in a `Padding`. This feels verbose at first, but it means any widget can be padded, centred or made tappable in exactly the same way.

```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

Widget tappableCentredLabel(VoidCallback onTap) {
  return Center(
    child: InkWell(
      onTap: onTap,
      child: const Padding(
        padding: EdgeInsets.all(16),
        child: Text('Tap me'),
      ),
    ),
  );
}
```

> TIP: When a `build` method gets long, extract parts into new widget classes (not helper methods). Separate widgets can be `const`, rebuild independently, and appear in the Flutter Inspector by name.

:::check Can you change a Text widget's text after it is created?
No. Widgets are immutable; Flutter creates a new Text widget with the new string on the next build.
:::

## Common mistakes
- Looking for properties like `padding` or `margin` on every widget instead of wrapping.
- Writing giant `build` methods instead of composing small widgets.

## Summary
- Widgets are immutable configuration objects; Flutter builds UIs from widget trees.
- Small widgets compose into larger ones.
- Layout, visual, interactive and structural widgets cover most needs.

## Further learning
- docs.flutter.dev ▸ UI ▸ Widget catalog
