@lesson flutter-m02-l02 | StatelessWidget | 12
## Learning objectives
- Create StatelessWidgets with constructor parameters
- Use `const` constructors and keys
- Know when a widget can be stateless

## Widgets without internal state
A `StatelessWidget` has no mutable state of its own. Everything it displays comes from its **constructor parameters** and from inherited data like the theme. Its `build` method returns the widgets to show.

```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

class LessonTile extends StatelessWidget {
  const LessonTile({
    super.key,
    required this.title,
    required this.minutes,
    this.completed = false,
    this.onTap,
  });

  final String title;
  final int minutes;
  final bool completed;
  final VoidCallback? onTap;

  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    return ListTile(
      leading: Icon(completed ? Icons.check_circle : Icons.play_circle_outline),
      title: Text(title),
      subtitle: Text('$minutes min'),
      onTap: onTap,
    );
  }
}
```

The parent decides what to pass in. When the parent rebuilds with different values (for example, `completed: true`), Flutter calls `LessonTile.build` again with the new configuration.

## Anatomy
- `extends StatelessWidget`
- A `const` constructor with `super.key`
- `final` fields for configuration
- `@override Widget build(BuildContext context)`

## const constructors
Mark the constructor `const` whenever all fields are final (they always are for widgets). Then callers can write `const LessonTile(title: 'Intro', minutes: 5)`, letting Flutter reuse the instance and skip rebuilding it.

## Keys
`super.key` forwards an optional `Key` to the base class. Keys help Flutter match widgets with their elements when lists reorder (covered later). Always accept the key parameter so callers can pass one.

## Stateless doesn't mean static
A stateless widget can display changing data: the data just lives somewhere else (a parent's state, a state-management object, a stream). It can also contain stateful children.

```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

class Greeting extends StatelessWidget {
  const Greeting({super.key, required this.name});
  final String name;

  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    final style = Theme.of(context).textTheme.headlineSmall;
    return Text('Welcome back, $name!', style: style);
  }
}
```

> TIP: Start every new widget as stateless. Convert it to stateful only when it truly needs to own changing data.

:::check Where does a StatelessWidget get the data it displays?
From its constructor parameters and inherited widgets such as Theme or MediaQuery.
:::

## Common mistakes
- Adding non-final fields to a StatelessWidget to "store" changing values; they're lost on rebuild.
- Forgetting `super.key` in the constructor.

## Summary
- StatelessWidgets render purely from their configuration and inherited data.
- Use const constructors and accept `super.key`.
- Prefer stateless widgets; keep mutable state elsewhere.

## Further learning
- api.flutter.dev ▸ StatelessWidget
@quiz
