@lesson flutter-m02-l05 | BuildContext | 12
## Learning objectives
- Explain what BuildContext represents
- Look up inherited data with `Theme.of`, `MediaQuery.of` and others
- Avoid common context mistakes

## Your position in the tree
Every `build` method receives a `BuildContext`. It is a handle to the widget's **location in the element tree**. Through it, a widget can look **up** the tree to find ancestors and inherited data.

```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

class ScreenInfo extends StatelessWidget {
  const ScreenInfo({super.key});

  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    final theme = Theme.of(context);            // nearest Theme
    final size = MediaQuery.sizeOf(context);    // screen size
    final colors = theme.colorScheme;
    return Container(
      color: colors.primaryContainer,
      padding: const EdgeInsets.all(16),
      child: Text(
        'Width: ${size.width.toStringAsFixed(0)} dp',
        style: theme.textTheme.titleMedium?.copyWith(color: colors.onPrimaryContainer),
      ),
    );
  }
}
```

## The `.of(context)` pattern
Many Flutter APIs follow the pattern `Something.of(context)`:
- `Theme.of(context)`: colours and text styles
- `MediaQuery.sizeOf(context)`, `MediaQuery.paddingOf(context)`: screen metrics
- `Navigator.of(context)`: push and pop routes
- `ScaffoldMessenger.of(context)`: show SnackBars
- `DefaultTextStyle.of(context)`, `Localizations.localeOf(context)`

These find the **nearest ancestor** of that type. Widgets that call `Theme.of(context)` are also **registered as dependents**, so they rebuild automatically when the theme changes.

## Context mistakes
**1. Using a context above the widget you're looking for.**

```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

class BadExample extends StatelessWidget {
  const BadExample({super.key});

  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    return Scaffold(
      body: Center(
        // Builder gives us a context *below* Scaffold.
        child: Builder(
          builder: (innerContext) => ElevatedButton(
            onPressed: () => ScaffoldMessenger.of(innerContext)
                .showSnackBar(const SnackBar(content: Text('Saved'))),
            child: const Text('Save'),
          ),
        ),
      ),
    );
  }
}
```

(`ScaffoldMessenger` is provided by `MaterialApp`, so this particular call works with either context, but the same issue applies to `Scaffold.of(context)` or a provider created in the same `build`: you need a context below it, which `Builder` or a separate widget gives you.)

**2. Using a context after an `await` when the widget may be gone.** Check `context.mounted` first:

```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

Future<void> saveAndClose(BuildContext context) async {
  await Future.delayed(const Duration(milliseconds: 300));
  if (!context.mounted) return;
  Navigator.of(context).pop();
}
```

> TIP: `MediaQuery.sizeOf(context)` rebuilds only when the size changes, unlike `MediaQuery.of(context).size`, which rebuilds on any MediaQuery change (keyboard, padding...). Prefer the specific methods.

:::check What does Theme.of(context) return when there are two Themes above the widget?
The nearest one (closest ancestor).
:::

## Common mistakes
- Looking up something that is created in the same build method with that build's own context.
- Using a stale context after async gaps without checking `context.mounted`.

## Summary
- BuildContext is a widget's location in the tree.
- `X.of(context)` finds the nearest ancestor and registers a dependency.
- Use `Builder` or a child widget for a context below a newly created ancestor; check `context.mounted` after `await`.

## Further learning
- api.flutter.dev ▸ BuildContext
