@lesson flutter-m02-l06 | The State lifecycle | 14
## Learning objectives
- Know the order of State lifecycle methods
- Initialise and dispose resources correctly
- React to configuration changes with didUpdateWidget

## Lifecycle at a glance
```diagram
createState()
     │
initState()            ← once: set up controllers, listeners
     │
didChangeDependencies()← after initState and when inherited data changes
     │
build()  ◀──────────── setState() / parent rebuild / dependency change
     │
didUpdateWidget(old)   ← parent rebuilt with a new widget configuration
     │
deactivate()           ← removed from the tree (may be reinserted)
     │
dispose()              ← permanently removed: release resources
```

## A complete example
```dart
import 'dart:async';
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

class CountdownText extends StatefulWidget {
  const CountdownText({super.key, required this.seconds});
  final int seconds;

  @override
  State<CountdownText> createState() => _CountdownTextState();
}

class _CountdownTextState extends State<CountdownText> {
  late int _remaining;
  Timer? _timer;

  @override
  void initState() {
    super.initState();               // always call super first
    _start();
  }

  @override
  void didUpdateWidget(CountdownText oldWidget) {
    super.didUpdateWidget(oldWidget);
    if (oldWidget.seconds != widget.seconds) _start(); // config changed: restart
  }

  void _start() {
    _timer?.cancel();
    _remaining = widget.seconds;
    _timer = Timer.periodic(const Duration(seconds: 1), (t) {
      if (_remaining <= 1) t.cancel();
      setState(() => _remaining--);
    });
  }

  @override
  void dispose() {
    _timer?.cancel();                // release resources
    super.dispose();                 // always call super last
  }

  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) => Text('$_remaining s');
}
```

## What goes where
- **initState**: create controllers (`TextEditingController`, `AnimationController`), subscribe to streams, start timers. You can't use `Theme.of(context)` or other inherited lookups here.
- **didChangeDependencies**: work that depends on inherited widgets (for example, reading `Localizations` or a provider once).
- **build**: describe UI only. No side effects, no network calls.
- **didUpdateWidget**: compare `oldWidget` with `widget` and react to changed parameters.
- **dispose**: cancel timers and subscriptions, dispose controllers and focus nodes.

> IMPORTANT: Call `super.initState()` first and `super.dispose()` last.

## Async work in initState
`initState` can't be `async`, but it can start async work:

```dart
// inside a State class
// @override
// void initState() {
//   super.initState();
//   _loadProfile(); // async method; check `mounted` before setState inside it
// }
```

:::check Can you call Theme.of(context) inside initState?
No. Inherited lookups aren't allowed in initState; use didChangeDependencies or build.
:::

## Common mistakes
- Forgetting to dispose controllers and cancel timers, leaking memory.
- Doing heavy work or network calls in `build`.

## Summary
- `initState` sets up, `didChangeDependencies` reacts to inherited data, `build` renders, `didUpdateWidget` handles new configs, `dispose` cleans up.
- Call `super` first in initState and last in dispose.
- Keep `build` pure; start async work from initState.

## Further learning
- api.flutter.dev ▸ State class ▸ Lifecycle
@quiz
? order: Put these lifecycle methods in the order they first run.
~ createState
~ initState
~ didChangeDependencies
~ build
> The State is created, initialised, receives dependencies, then builds.
? mcq: Where should you dispose a TextEditingController?
+ In dispose()
- In build()
- In initState()
- Nowhere; it disposes itself
> Controllers hold resources that must be released in dispose.
? tf: initState can be marked async so you can await inside it.
= false
> initState must be synchronous; start async work from it without awaiting.
? fill: The lifecycle method called when the parent rebuilds with a new configuration is did___Widget.
= Update
> didUpdateWidget(oldWidget) lets you compare configurations.
? scenario: A countdown widget's `seconds` parameter changes from 10 to 30, but the timer keeps counting from the old value. Which method should handle this?
+ didUpdateWidget, comparing oldWidget.seconds with widget.seconds
- dispose
- createState
- build, by restarting the timer every frame
> didUpdateWidget reacts to configuration changes.

@lesson flutter-m02-l07 | Hot reload | 10
## Learning objectives
- Explain how hot reload works
- Know which changes hot reload can and can't apply
- Use hot reload effectively during UI development

## Change code, keep state
**Hot reload** injects updated Dart source into the running app (using the JIT-compiled Dart VM in debug mode), then rebuilds the widget tree. Your app **keeps its state**: you stay on the same screen with the same data, and see your change in about a second.

Trigger it by saving in your IDE, pressing the ⚡ button, or typing `r` in the `flutter run` terminal.

## What hot reload applies
After reload, Flutter calls `build` again on widgets, so anything evaluated **during build** reflects your changes:

```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

class Banner2 extends StatelessWidget {
  const Banner2({super.key});

  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    // Change the text or colour, save, and you'll see it immediately.
    return Container(
      color: Colors.indigo,
      padding: const EdgeInsets.all(24),
      child: const Text('Learn Flutter', style: TextStyle(color: Colors.white, fontSize: 24)),
    );
  }
}
```

## What hot reload can't apply
Code that already ran and won't run again isn't affected:
- Changes to `main()` or code that runs before `runApp`
- Changes to `initState()` for widgets already created
- Initialisers of global or static variables (they keep their old values)
- Changing a StatelessWidget to a StatefulWidget (or vice versa) may require a restart
- Changes to enum or generic type declarations in some cases
- Native code (Kotlin, Swift, Gradle, Info.plist) or new plugins

In these cases use **hot restart** (next lesson) or a full rebuild.

## Debug only
Hot reload works only in **debug mode** (JIT). Profile and release builds are AOT-compiled for speed and don't support it.

## Tips for fast UI iteration
- Keep the screen you're working on open; hot reload preserves navigation state.
- Temporarily set your screen as `home:` while designing it.
- Use the Inspector's "Select widget" mode to jump from a widget on screen to its code.

> TIP: If hot reload prints "Reloaded 0 libraries", the file you edited wasn't saved or isn't part of the app.

:::check Why doesn't hot reload show a change you made inside initState?
initState already ran for the existing State; hot reload rebuilds widgets but doesn't re-run initState.
:::

## Common mistakes
- Expecting hot reload to reset counters or reload data; it intentionally keeps state.
- Debugging "bugs" that are actually stale state from many reloads; hot restart to get a clean slate.

## Summary
- Hot reload injects new code and rebuilds while keeping state.
- It doesn't re-run main, initState or global initialisers, nor apply native changes.
- It works only in debug mode.

## Further learning
- docs.flutter.dev ▸ Tools ▸ Hot reload
