@lesson flutter-m02-l08 | Hot restart | 8
## Learning objectives
- Distinguish hot restart from hot reload and full restart
- Choose the right one for each change
- Troubleshoot stale state

## Restart the Dart side
**Hot restart** reloads your code and **restarts the Dart application from `main()`**, clearing all state, without rebuilding the native app. It takes a few seconds, much faster than stopping and running again.

Trigger it with `R` in the terminal, the restart button in your IDE, or `Ctrl+Shift+F5` / `⇧⌘F5` in VS Code.

## The three levels
- **Hot reload**: about a second. Applies code changes, keeps state. Use for UI tweaks and logic in build or event handlers.
- **Hot restart**: a few seconds. Re-runs `main()`, resets all Dart state. Use for changes to `main`, `initState`, globals, enums, app initialisation, or when state is messed up.
- **Full restart / rebuild**: tens of seconds or more. Recompiles native code. Use after adding plugins, editing Gradle/Kotlin/Swift, the manifest or Info.plist, or assets that require native packaging.

## Example: when hot restart is needed
```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

late final String flavour; // initialised once at startup

void main() {
  flavour = 'staging'; // change to 'production' → needs hot restart
  runApp(MaterialApp(home: Scaffold(body: Center(child: Text('Flavour: $flavour')))));
}
```

Changing `'staging'` and hot reloading does nothing, because `main` doesn't run again. Hot restart runs it, so the new value appears.

## Assets
Adding a new image to an already-declared assets folder usually works with hot restart (sometimes even hot reload). Changing the `assets:` section of `pubspec.yaml` requires stopping and running the app again.

> TIP: When behaviour seems impossible ("this should work!"), hot restart before debugging. Stale state from many hot reloads is a common source of confusion.

:::check Does hot restart reinstall the native app?
No. It restarts the Dart side only; native code isn't rebuilt.
:::

## Common mistakes
- Running a full rebuild for every change, wasting minutes.
- Expecting hot restart to pick up new plugins.

## Summary
- Hot restart re-runs `main()` and clears state without a native rebuild.
- Use reload for UI tweaks, restart for startup/global changes, full rebuild for native changes.

## Further learning
- docs.flutter.dev ▸ Tools ▸ Hot reload ▸ Special cases
